# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.
This file follows the [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.0.0/) format
and adheres to [SemVer](https://semver.org/) versioning.

---

## [0.21.29] - 2026-10-30

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** Three fixes, the `v2` ports of #386, #407 and
#362 from `main`: a `UNIQUE` index on the identifier of USER tables in
generated migrations (#397, #420), `SearchHighlight` tags written as SurrealQL
string literals (#413, #431), and an explicit sign-up set in the generated
`SIGNUP` clause (#366, #432). Read *Behaviour change* and *Migration*. The
SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range
2.6.x – 2.7.x).

`SurrealConfigDict(signup_fields=[...])` is the one new public name. On the
LTS line it is part of the #366 fix (the sign-up set must be declared for the
generated clause to assign anything beyond the identifier and the password),
not a feature, so this stays a patch release.

### Fixed

- **`makemigrations` / `schema_diff()` add a `UNIQUE` index on the identifier
  field of every `TableType.USER` table (#397, backport of #386):**
  `DEFINE INDEX <table>_<identifier>_unique ON <table> FIELDS <identifier> UNIQUE`
  (`email` by default, `identifier_field` honoured). A second `signup()` with
  an identifier already in use is rejected by the database instead of
  creating a second account that `SIGNIN` cannot tell apart.
- **`schema_diff()` / `makemigrations` keep an equivalent `UNIQUE` index under
  another name (#397).** When the database already enforces the same
  single-field `UNIQUE` index under another name, the diff no longer proposes
  a second index plus a `DropIndex`. Other indexes are still matched by name.
- **`SearchHighlight` writes both tags with `surql_string_literal()` (#413,
  backport of #407)**, backslash escaped first, then the apostrophe, so a tag
  containing a backslash reaches `search::highlight()` as written. Before,
  only the apostrophe was escaped: a tag ending in a backslash made the query
  fail to parse, and a tag holding an escape such as `\n` was highlighted with
  a newline instead of the two characters written.
- **The generated `DEFINE ACCESS ... SIGNUP` clause assigns only an explicit
  sign-up set (#366, v2 port of #333):** the identifier, the hashed password,
  the fields listed in the new `SurrealConfigDict(signup_fields=[...])` and,
  when the model has that field, `created_at = time::now()`. Every other field
  keeps its schema `DEFAULT` or model default. Before, every model field was
  assigned from a `$field` parameter, and `created_at = time::now()` was added
  even to models without that field. A `signup_fields` entry that is `id`, the
  identifier, the password, `created_at`, a computed field, a `server_fields`
  entry, a read-only field or an unknown name raises `ValueError` when the
  access definition is built.
- **The migration introspector builds the access definition with the same
  builder as `AccessGenerator.from_model()` (#366)**, so `makemigrations` /
  `schemadiff` use `encryption_algorithm` and `access_name` instead of
  hard-coding `argon2` and `'<table>_auth'`.

### Behaviour change

- **One more `DEFINE INDEX` per USER table** on the next `makemigrations` /
  `schema_diff()` against an existing database (#397). `define_table()` and
  `define_access()` do not create the index.
- **`AuthenticatedUserMixin.signup()` raises `SurrealDbError`, before any
  request, for a keyword outside the identifier, the password and
  `signup_fields` (#366).** A sign-up request that carries an unlisted field
  no longer sets it.
- **One `REMOVE ACCESS` + `DEFINE ACCESS` per USER model** on the next
  `makemigrations` / `schema_diff()` (#366). Where `access_name` is set, the
  access is now created under that name, not `'<table>_auth'`.
- **`SearchHighlight.ref` is converted with `int()` when the query is
  rendered (#413).** An `int` subclass renders as its number; a value `int()`
  cannot convert raises `ValueError` or `TypeError`.

### Migration

- **USER tables with duplicate identifiers (#397):** the new index fails to
  apply (`MigrationStatementError`; SurrealDB 2.x reports
  ``Database index `...` already contains '...', with record `...` ``) while
  the table holds duplicates. List them first, replacing the table and the
  identifier field with your own:
  `SELECT * FROM (SELECT email, count() AS n FROM User GROUP BY email) WHERE n > 1;`,
  resolve them, then apply the migration.
- **Sign-up fields (#366):** list in `signup_fields` the fields your sign-up
  form sets (for example `signup_fields=["name"]`), then re-apply the access
  definition.

### Known limitations

- `SearchScore.to_surql()` still writes `ref` as given, without `int()`
  (same as `main`).

## [0.21.28] - 2026-10-29

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS), backfilled.** Published automatically by the
dependency sync (#428, #429) with the version files only; it also shipped
three fixes merged since 0.21.27, the `v2` ports of #396, #334 and #367 from
`main`: `HTTPTransaction.commit()` raises when the server rejects a statement
(#399, #411), `HTTPTransaction` data keys must be identifiers (#382, #414),
and filter keys and `values()` names must be field names (#377, #416). Read
*Behaviour change* and *Migration*. The SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested
against 2.7.0).

### Fixed

- **`HTTPTransaction.commit()` raises `TransactionError` when SurrealDB
  rejects a statement of the batch (#399, backport of #396).** The server
  cancels the whole batch and writes nothing, but `is_committed` used to be
  `True`. The error carries the rejected statement's own server error; after
  the failure `is_committed` is `False` and the transaction is inactive.
- **A retryable conflict in an HTTP transaction commit raises
  `TransactionConflictError` (#399)**, so `@retry_on_conflict` retries it. The
  generic "not executed due to a failed transaction" cancellation message is
  no longer mistaken for a conflict. When the commit request itself fails, the
  `TransactionError` chains the original exception.
- **`HTTPTransaction.create/insert/update/upsert/merge/relate` check each key
  of `data` when the statement is queued (#382, backport of #334).** A key
  that is not a plain SurrealQL identifier (`[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`) raises
  `ValueError` naming the key and nothing is queued; `insert()` checks every
  record before queueing any.
- **`QuerySet.filter()`, `Q` and relation filter keys, and
  `QuerySet.values()` names, must be a field name or a dotted path of field
  names (#377, backport of #367)** (`"address.city"`,
  `"address.zip__startswith"`). Anything else (for example `"name x"` or
  `"a-b"`) raises `ValueError` naming the key before any query is sent.
  Before, such a key reached the server as written.
- **Trailing whitespace is no longer accepted in identifiers (#377).** A name
  ending in a newline passed `validate_identifier()` and the other identifier
  checks (model field and relation names, `bulk_update()` keys,
  `atomic_append()` / `atomic_remove()` / `atomic_set_add()` fields, SDK live
  query / live select / change feed table names). These checks now require a
  full match. `format_thing()` backtick-escapes a record ID ending in a
  newline.

### Behaviour change

- **HTTP transactions raise on a rejected statement (#399).** Over HTTP,
  `async with SurrealDBConnectionManager.transaction()`, ORM `save(tx=)`,
  `update(tx=)`, `merge(tx=)` and the `atomic=True` bulk operations now raise
  `TransactionError` when the server rejects a statement. Before, they
  silently did nothing. WebSocket transactions, successful batches and empty
  transactions are unchanged.
- **A non-identifier `HTTPTransaction` data key raises `ValueError` at the
  call (#382)**; it used to fail only at `commit()` (`TransactionError`).
  Valid keys produce exactly the same SurrealQL. `WebSocketTransaction` is
  unchanged.
- **An unknown filter lookup raises `ValueError` (#377)** (for example
  `name__iexact`); it used to be rendered silently as `=`. Relation filters
  render only `exact`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, `in`, `contains`,
  `startswith`, `endswith` and `isnull`; any other lookup now raises
  `ValueError` instead of falling back to `=`.

### Migration

- Code that ran HTTP transactions and assumed success will now see a
  `TransactionError` (or `TransactionConflictError`) where a write was
  rejected: catch it, or wrap the call in `@retry_on_conflict` for conflicts.
- Rename any non-identifier key passed to an `HTTPTransaction` method.
- Replace any unsupported lookup with a supported one (the error message
  lists them); use plain field names or dotted paths in `filter()` and
  `values()`, and `raw_query()` for expressions.

### Dependencies

- **`tornado` 6.5.9 → 6.6 in `uv.lock` (#428, sync of #426 from `main`)** —
  GHSA-r4j5-9xpp-3985, GHSA-pw9p-v7r6-gx4p and GHSA-pcg2-g6cj-q92c.
  `tornado` reaches the lock only through the notebook tooling of the dev
  group; it is not a dependency of the published package.

### Known limitations

- `HTTPTransaction.insert()` iterates `data` twice (validation, then
  queueing), so a generator passed instead of the documented list or dict
  queues nothing (same as `main`).

## [0.21.27] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One fix, the `v2` port of #283 from `main`:
string literals in migration DDL and schema introspection (#284, #395). Read
*Behaviour change* and *Migration* if your models or migration files pass a
server-side expression such as `"time::now()"` as a plain `str` default. The
SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range
2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **Migration DDL renders a `COMMENT` or a string `DEFAULT` containing an
  apostrophe or a backslash with SurrealQL's backslash escapes**
  (`'it\'s'`, `'C:\\temp'`). Before, the apostrophe was doubled
  (`'it''s'`), which SurrealDB 2.x does not read as one quote: the literal
  ended early and the statement failed to parse. A backslash was not escaped,
  so `'C:\temp'` was stored as a different text. This covers field and table
  `COMMENT`, string `DEFAULT`, and the `COMMENT` of `CreateIndex`,
  `DefineAccess` and `DefineEvent` (#284).
- **Schema introspection reads back the double-quoted strings SurrealDB
  reports** for any text containing an apostrophe, and decodes their
  escapes, for field and table `COMMENT` and quoted `DEFAULT` values. Before,
  the quotes and escapes were kept, so `schema_diff()` / `makemigrations`
  kept proposing changes, and an apostrophe in a comment made the parser
  drop every clause after it, `PERMISSIONS` included (#284).
- **`inspectdb` writes every string default with `repr()`.** A default
  containing `::` or `(` used to be written as `"<text>"` without escaping,
  which is invalid Python for a text containing `"` (#284).
- **`surreal_orm.utils.escape_single_quotes()` escapes with backslashes**
  instead of doubling the apostrophe. The new
  `surreal_orm.utils.surql_string_literal()` renders a whole single-quoted
  SurrealQL literal (#284).

### Behaviour change

- **Migration string literals:** the DDL generated for a comment or string
  default containing `'` or `\` is different (escaped with a backslash).
  Those already failed or were stored with different text; every other
  literal renders as before.
- **A `str` default is always written as a quoted string.** Server-side
  expressions are carried as `SurrealFunc`: the parser, `makemigrations` and
  `inspectdb` emit `SurrealFunc(...)` for an unquoted `DEFAULT`. Before, any
  `str` containing `::` or starting with `$` was written unquoted, so a text
  default such as `'fr::CA'` did not round-trip.
- **Compatibility:** a `str` that is exactly a no-argument function call
  (`"time::now()"`, `"rand::uuid()"`) or a bare `$parameter` is still
  written as an expression, with a `DeprecationWarning`. Any other `str`
  containing `::` or starting with `$` (for example `"time::now() + 1d"`) is
  now stored as text. The same rule applies to model defaults:
  `Field(default="time::now()")` is introspected as
  `SurrealFunc("time::now()")`, so `schema_diff()` / `makemigrations`
  converge for it.

### Migration

- In models and existing migration files, wrap expression values of
  `default=` / `previous_default=` in `SurrealFunc(...)`
  (`from surreal_orm import SurrealFunc`) to silence the
  `DeprecationWarning`. Expressions with arguments or operators must be
  wrapped, or they are written as text.

## [0.21.26] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** Three fixes, the `v2` ports of #356, #365 and
#370 from `main`: `QueryCache` invalidation on write reaches every connection
(#358, #384), pagination and KNN arguments accept only integers (#368, #385),
and the `validate_token()` cache is bounded by the token's `exp`, the model
table and the connection, with auth calls following the model's connection
routing (#372, #389). Read *Behaviour change* and *Migration*. The SurrealDB
pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **`QueryCache` invalidation after a write reaches every connection (#358).**
  Since 0.21.23 (#335), a write (`save()`, `merge()`, `update()` or a removal) only
  dropped the cached entries of the writing connection's location (URL,
  namespace, database). The same database reached through two URL spellings
  (a trailing `/`, `http` vs `ws`, `localhost` vs `127.0.0.1`), or a
  `save(tx=...)` running on another connection than the model's routing, could
  keep stale `.cache()` results until their TTL expired. A write now drops the
  written table's entries for every connection; reads are still cached per
  connection.
- **`QuerySet.limit()`, `offset()`, `similar_to(limit, ef)` and
  `hybrid_search(vector_limit, text_limit, rrf_k)` accept only integers
  (#368).** The value used to be written into the query text as given, so a
  numeric string, a float or a bool produced a malformed query, or one that
  did something other than what the call described. Any other type now raises
  `TypeError` at the call, and a value below the minimum raises `ValueError`
  (0 for `limit`, `offset` and `ef`; 1 for the KNN `limit`, `vector_limit`,
  `text_limit` and `rrf_k`). In both cases no query is sent. Valid integers
  render exactly as before, and `ef=None` is still accepted.
- **`validate_token()` no longer returns a cached result after the token's own
  `exp` (#372).** A cache entry now lasts until the earlier of the configured
  TTL and the token's `exp`. Before, a cache hit only checked the cache TTL
  (300 s by default).
- **`validate_token()` and `authenticate_token()` return `None` when the
  token's `$auth` record is not in the calling model's table**, whether the
  result is fresh or cached (#372).
- **`validate_token()` cache entries are no longer shared between
  connections.** The cache is keyed by connection (name, URL, namespace,
  database), model table and token. `invalidate_token_cache(token)` removes the
  token's entries for every model table and every connection (#372).
- **Auth calls on `AuthenticatedUserMixin` models follow the model's
  `connection=` setting and `SurrealDBConnectionManager.using()`** —
  `signup`, `signin`, `authenticate_token`, `validate_token` and
  `change_password`. Before, with the documented
  `class User(AuthenticatedUserMixin, BaseSurrealModel)` order, the mixin's
  runtime `get_connection_name()` stub shadowed the model's and they always
  used the `default` connection (#372).

### Behaviour change

- A write now invalidates the written table's `.cache()` entries on all
  connections, which can mean more cache misses after a write, never a stale
  hit.
- A non-integer value passed to `limit()`, `offset()`, `similar_to()` or
  `hybrid_search()` now raises at the call instead of being written into the
  query.
- A token whose `exp` claim cannot be read (opaque token, no `exp` claim, or a
  non-numeric one) is validated against the server on every call and never
  cached.
- Validating a token through a model other than the one whose table issued it
  returns `None`: validate it through the model it belongs to.
- A mixin-first model declared with `connection="x"`, or used inside
  `using("x")`, sends its auth calls to connection `x` instead of `default`.

### Migration

- No schema, DDL or public signature change.
- Convert values from request parameters or other untyped sources with
  `int(...)` before passing them, e.g. `qs.limit(int(page_size))`.
- Code that read or wrote the private `AuthenticatedUserMixin._token_cache`
  directly must use the new `(connection identity, table, token)` key; use
  `invalidate_token_cache()` instead.
- If your access is defined only on the `default` database while the model is
  routed elsewhere, define the access on the routed database or remove the
  `connection=` setting.

### Known limitation

- `validate_token_local()` (local decode, no network call) does not apply the
  new model-table check: it still returns the record id whatever its table.
  Use `validate_token()` when the table matters.

## [0.21.25] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** Three fixes, the `v2` ports of #347, #341 and
#351 from `main`: the HTTP connection signs in again only for the identity it
signed in as (#349, #376), `$`-prefixed filter values are bound unless they
name a declared variable (#342, #379), and `signin()` / `signup()` /
`change_password()` act on the session's own record (#353, #381). Read
*Behaviour change* and *Migration*. The SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested
against 2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **`HTTPConnection` signs in again only for the identity it signed in as
  (#349).** The transient-`401` recovery (#101) re-mints a token with the
  arguments of the last successful `signin()`. Nothing cleared them, so when a
  token installed by `authenticate()` or `signup()` was rejected (for example
  once it ran out), the connection signed in again with the earlier
  credentials and the request went through under that earlier identity. The
  request now fails with `QueryError` (code 401). The retained arguments are
  also cleared by `authenticate()`, `signup()`, a failed `signin()` and
  `close()`. Recovery after `signin()` is unchanged.
- **`$`-prefixed filter values are bound unless they name a declared variable
  (#342).** A `QuerySet` filter value starting with `$` was always written into
  the query as a variable reference, so a value that merely starts with `$`
  (an email, a search term) changed the condition instead of being matched as
  text. A value is now a reference only when it is exactly `$name` and `name`
  was declared with `.variables()` on the QuerySet (or on the inner QuerySet of
  a `Subquery`); every other value is bound as a parameter. This covers keyword
  filters, `Q` / `~Q`, `Subquery` in filters and `annotate()`, `live()`,
  `count()`, the bulk operations and `ForeignKey` filter values.
- **Variables declared on a `Subquery`'s inner QuerySet or on a `Prefetch`
  queryset are sent with the query (#342).** A reference to them used to reach
  the server unbound.
- **`signin()` and `signup()` return the session's own record (#353).**
  `AuthenticatedUserMixin` looked the user up a second time by
  `identifier_field` and took the first row, so when several records shared an
  identifier value the returned user could be a different record from the one
  the token was issued for. They now read `$auth` on the sign-in connection and
  fetch that record by ID.
- **`change_password()` updates only the record that signed in (#353).** It
  used to update every record sharing the identifier value.
- **`authenticate_token()` finds records whose key looks like a number
  (#353).** The record ID is bound as decoded, so `users:⟨000000⟩` is found
  instead of returning `None`.

### Added

- **`HTTPConnection.invalidate()`** sends the `invalidate` RPC, then clears
  the token, the authenticated flag and the retained sign-in arguments, even
  when the RPC fails (that error is raised). Later requests carry no
  credentials until the next `signin()` or `authenticate()`.

### Behaviour change

- On an HTTP connection whose token came from `authenticate()` or `signup()`,
  a `401` is now raised as `QueryError` (code 401) instead of being answered by
  signing in again with credentials from an earlier `signin()`.
- `filter(name="$undeclared")` now matches records whose field is literally
  `"$undeclared"`; it used to compare the field against an unset variable. Any
  `$`-prefixed value that is not exactly a declared name (trailing text, a
  trailing newline, `$auth.id`, a name such as `$_f0`) is compared as text. On
  a `ForeignKey` field such a value is treated as a bare ID of the target
  table. Built-in server parameters such as `$auth` can no longer be referenced
  from `filter()`.
- `signin()` / `signup()` raise `SurrealDbError` if `$auth` cannot be read
  after a successful access call, and `DoesNotExist` if that record no longer
  exists; there is no fallback to the identifier lookup. `identifier_field` no
  longer has to be among the credentials passed to them. `change_password()`
  raises `SurrealDbError` unless exactly the signed-in record was updated.
  Nothing changes for applications whose identifier values are unique; the
  `(user, token)` return shape is unchanged.

### Migration

- To keep an HTTP connection usable past a record token's lifetime, call
  `authenticate()` again with a fresh token, or call `signin()` again
  explicitly.
- If a filter relied on a `$name` never passed to `.variables()`, declare it:
  `.filter(age__gte="$min_age").variables(min_age=18)`. Use `raw_query()` for
  built-in server parameters such as `$auth`.

## [0.21.24] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** Two SDK fixes, the `v2` ports of #318 and #302
from `main`: HTTP transactions parse record references strictly and bind
non-identifier IDs (#322, #337), and change feeds work on SurrealDB 2.x
(#308, #339). Read *Behaviour change* and *Migration*. The SurrealDB pin is
unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **`HTTPTransaction` record references are parsed strictly (#322).**
  `create` / `update` / `upsert` / `merge` / `delete` / `relate` now parse
  `table:id` references strictly. A record ID that is not a plain identifier,
  a signed 64-bit integer or `rand()` / `ulid()` / `uuid()` is bound as a
  parameter (`type::thing('table', $_ref)`). Previously, IDs with a space,
  `-`, `;`, a backtick, a backslash or a leading digit failed to parse, and
  `users:a-b` parsed as an expression and wrote nothing. The `format_thing()`
  backtick form is read back once (never escaped twice). `relate()`'s relation
  and `insert()`'s table must be identifiers. `WebSocketTransaction` applies
  the same checks before each call.
- **`HTTPTransaction.merge()` did nothing (#322).** It rendered
  `MERGE { k = $k }`, which the server rejects, so a merge queued in an HTTP
  transaction — including ORM `save(tx=)` / `merge(tx=)` over HTTP — was
  dropped. It now renders `MERGE { k: $k }`.
- **Change feeds work on SurrealDB 2.x (#308).** `ChangeFeedStream` sent
  `SHOW CHANGES ... SINCE '<text>'`, which SurrealDB 2.7.0 rejects at parse
  time, and the polling loops swallowed the error, so `QuerySet.changes()`
  never delivered a change. `since` is now parsed and rendered as `d'<UTC>'`
  or an unquoted versionstamp, converted to the 2.x `SINCE` scale (commit
  version = versionstamp >> 16).
- **Each change-feed entry is delivered once.** The cursor is the last
  versionstamp + 1 and can be passed back as `since`. Entries of other tables
  no longer stall a stream (the server applies `LIMIT` before the table
  filter).
- **The default `since` starts after the last entry already in the feed**;
  a datetime "now" would have replayed the whole feed on 2.x. It now starts
  even while other tables are being written (finding the starting point could
  wait forever), and `get_changes()` returns under concurrent writes.
- **`MultiTableChangeFeed.stream()`** keeps an advancing cursor per table,
  honours `since` and no longer drops errors.
- **`ChangeModelStream`** delivers updates on `CHANGEFEED ... INCLUDE ORIGINAL`
  tables. Its `cursor` stays on a transaction until the transaction's last
  event is yielded (a resume taken mid-transaction delivers that transaction
  again in full: at-least-once).

### Behaviour change

- A malformed transaction reference (`':x'`, `'a b:c'`, an empty table or ID,
  an array or object ID, an unclosed delimiter, a trailing newline) now raises
  `ValueError` before anything is queued or sent; it used to reach the server.
  ORM transactional merges over HTTP used to do nothing and now write the data.
- A failed request or a statement the server rejects now raises
  `ChangeFeedError` from `get_changes()`, `stream()`, `stream_batch()`,
  `MultiTableChangeFeed.stream()` and `QuerySet.changes()` iteration, instead
  of being retried silently forever. An invalid `since` raises `ValueError`
  (`TypeError` for an unsupported type) before any query is sent.
- A change-feed poll that reaches the end of the feed without a new entry for
  the table now sleeps `poll_interval`, even when other tables wrote in the
  meantime; before, those writes made it poll again with no pause.

### Migration

- Callers that passed array or object record IDs to transaction methods must
  use `query()` instead. Plain-identifier, integer and table-only references
  produce the same SQL as before.
- `since` accepts `None` (from now on), an `int` or decimal-string
  versionstamp (inclusive; `0` reads the whole retained feed), a `datetime`
  (a naive one is read as UTC), or an ISO 8601 date or date-time string. A
  cursor saved by an earlier version cannot be reused, because those streams
  never produced one. On SurrealDB 2.x a datetime start may return earlier
  changes, so prefer a versionstamp to resume.

## [0.21.23] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One ORM fix (#315, #335), the `v2` port of #309
from `main`: `QueryCache` entries are keyed by the connection a query runs on.
Read *Behaviour change*. The SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0,
supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **`.cache()` queries no longer share entries across connections (#315).**
  The same cached query run under different named connections (`using()`,
  `SurrealConfigDict(connection=...)`) pointing at different databases returned
  the rows cached from the first one. Cache keys now include the effective
  connection's name, URL, namespace and database (never credentials).
- **Writes invalidate only the database they went to.** A `save()` /
  `update()` / `delete()` drops the table's cached entries for that database,
  under every connection name pointing at it, plus the table's entries stored
  without a connection. Cached results for other databases stay in place.

### Behaviour change

- Each connection identity (name, URL, namespace, database) now has its own
  cache entries; previously one cached result was served to every connection
  running the same query on the same table.
- `QueryCache.make_key()` and `QueryCache.set()` gain an optional keyword-only
  `connection=` argument. Calls without it keep working. `invalidate(Model)`,
  `clear()` and `stats()` are unchanged.

### Migration

- None. The cache lives in memory only, so nothing persisted changes shape.

## [0.21.22] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One ORM fix (#310, #320), the `v2` port of #301
from `main`: record IDs containing a backtick or a backslash round-trip. One
small SDK helper is added. Read *Behaviour change*. The SurrealDB pin is
unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **Record IDs containing a backtick or a backslash now round-trip (#310).**
  Every ORM path that writes the ID into the query text — `merge()` with a
  `SurrealFunc`, `atomic_append()` / `atomic_remove()` / `atomic_set_add()`,
  `relate()`, `get_related()`, `remove_relation()`, `remove_all_relations()`,
  graph traversals and `delete()` — used to fail with a parse error on
  SurrealDB 2.x, or name a different record. This includes an ID ending in a
  backslash. `escape_record_id()` now writes SurrealDB's `` \` `` and `\\`
  escape sequences instead of doubling the backtick (checked on 2.7.0: both
  read back as the original character, and a doubled backtick does not parse).
- `RelationQuerySet` builds its start node with `format_thing()`, like every
  other caller.

### Added

- `surreal_sdk.protocol.cbor.unescape_record_id()`, the inverse of
  `escape_record_id()`. `parse_record_id()` and the SDK connections' CBOR
  thing conversion both use it.

### Behaviour change

- Only record IDs containing `` ` `` or `\` produce different SurrealQL.
  Plain and digit-leading IDs are written exactly as before.
- `parse_record_id()` and the SDK connections' handling of `table:id` strings
  on HTTP and WebSocket now decode `\\` and `` \` `` inside a
  backtick-delimited ID: ``t:`a\\b` `` now addresses the ID `a\b`, where it
  used to address `a\\b`. A doubled backtick is still read as one backtick,
  and any other backslash is kept as written.

### Migration note

- None.

---

## [0.21.21] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One CLI fix (#303, #317), the `v2` port of #298
from `main`. No new public API. The SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against
2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **CLI database commands failed on Python 3.14 (#303).** The CLI ran each
  command's coroutine with `asyncio.get_event_loop().run_until_complete()`. A
  click command starts with no current event loop: Python 3.12 – 3.13 created
  one with a `DeprecationWarning`, and Python 3.14 raises `RuntimeError`
  ("There is no current event loop"), so `makemigrations` (reading the schema),
  `migrate`, `upgrade`, `rollback`, `status`, `sqlmigrate`, `inspectdb`,
  `schemadiff` and `shell` failed before doing anything. The CLI now runs its
  coroutines with `asyncio.run()`.
- **`surreal-orm shell`** keeps a single `asyncio.Runner` for the session and
  runs the blocking prompt outside it, so Ctrl+C at the prompt is no longer
  turned into the cancellation of a running coroutine.

### Behaviour change

- Each CLI command runs on a fresh event loop that is closed when the command
  returns. No public API change.

### Migration note

- None.

---

## [0.21.20] - 2026-10-28

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One ORM fix (#294, #307), the `v2` port of #286
from `main`: an optional `ForeignKey` is now converted to a record link like a
required one. No new public API. Read *Behaviour change* and *Migration note*.
The SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range
2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **An optional `ForeignKey` was never converted to a record link (#294).**
  With `ForeignKey("X") | None` or `Optional[ForeignKey("X")]`, `save()`,
  `update()`, `merge()` and `bulk_update()` sent the value as a string, so
  SurrealDB 2.x rejected the write on an `option<record<...>>` column and a
  SCHEMALESS table stored the string instead of a link. `filter()` (`exact` /
  `in` / `not_in`, including inside `Q`) bound a string and silently returned no
  rows for records that exist. `get_foreign_key_targets()` read the marker from
  `FieldInfo.metadata` only, and Pydantic leaves a union member's `Annotated`
  marker on `FieldInfo.annotation`. It now finds the marker inside the union,
  and `get_foreign_key_columns()` with it.

### Behaviour change

- Optional `ForeignKey` fields are now written as record links and bound as
  `RecordId` in `exact` / `in` / `not_in` filters, the same as required
  `ForeignKey` fields since 0.21.5. A model instance, a full `"table:id"` and a
  bare id are all accepted. `None`, `isnull`, `$var` references and string
  lookups are unchanged.

### Migration note

- Rows written through an optional `ForeignKey` before this release, into a
  column that accepted a string (a SCHEMALESS table, or a field typed `any`),
  hold a string: `'table:id'`, or the bare id when one was passed. A filter on
  that field no longer matches them. Convert them once (SurrealDB 2.x syntax,
  checked on 2.7.0):

  ```sql
  UPDATE <table> SET <field> = IF string::contains(<field>, ':')
      THEN type::thing(<field>)
      ELSE type::thing('<target_table>', <field>) END
  WHERE type::is::string(<field>);
  ```

  `type::thing(<field>)` alone fails on a bare id, and the whole statement then
  converts nothing.
- A field declared `option<string>` now rejects the write: retype it
  `option<record<target_table>>`. No DDL change in the ORM.

### Known limitations

- The exported helpers `is_foreign_key()`, `is_relation_field()`,
  `is_many_to_many()`, `is_graph_relation()` and `get_relation_info()` still
  look only at a top-level `Annotated`, so they report `ForeignKey("X") | None`
  as not a relation, as in earlier releases. The migrations introspector has the
  same union gap. Both are separate defects.

---

## [0.21.19] - 2026-10-27

**Security release (V2 LTS).** The declared `aiohttp` floor moves from
`>=3.14.0` to `>=3.14.4` (#245, #293). No library code change. The SurrealDB pin
is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Security

- **The declared `aiohttp` floor is raised to `>=3.14.4` (#245, #293)**, in
  both the package metadata and the bundled SDK's `pyproject.toml`. The `v2`
  lock has resolved `aiohttp` 3.14.5 since 0.21.10, but downstream installs
  read only the declared requirement, so `pip install -U surrealdb-orm` and
  existing downstream lockfiles could keep an `aiohttp` 3.14.0 – 3.14.3.
  3.14.4 is the first release patching GHSA-494j-54xm-rwp3,
  GHSA-2g87-pp6c-29x5, GHSA-5wr8-gcqf-gqf3, GHSA-f9xw-jh8m-3mwq,
  GHSA-gx76-c99x-2c96, GHSA-6cjc-53gc-5rqm, GHSA-pqxq-w44r-73xw and
  GHSA-9xw5-w5jj-pvcg, which all affect `aiohttp <= 3.14.3`.
- The new floor also excludes `aiohttp` 3.14.0 – 3.14.2, which the old floor
  still admitted, and with them the 11 advisories fixed in 3.14.1 – 3.14.3:
  GHSA-2fqr-mr3j-6wp8, GHSA-4fvr-rgm6-gqmc, GHSA-4m7w-qmgq-4wj5,
  GHSA-63hw-fmq6-xxg2, GHSA-9x8q-7h8h-wcw9, GHSA-g3cq-j2xw-wf74,
  GHSA-hpj7-wq8m-9hgp and GHSA-xcgm-r5h9-7989 (fixed in 3.14.1),
  GHSA-mfx4-hv73-q22v and GHSA-mq44-7p77-q5h7 (fixed in 3.14.2), and
  GHSA-cq5v-8q36-5273 (fixed in 3.14.3). Users still on 3.14.0 – 3.14.2 are
  covered by the same upgrade.
- The lock is unchanged: `aiohttp` stays at 3.14.5 and `cbor2` stays at 5.9.0
  (`<6` on this line).

### Behaviour change

- Installing or upgrading `surrealdb-orm` now requires `aiohttp >= 3.14.4`. An
  environment that pins `aiohttp < 3.14.4` now fails at resolution time instead
  of installing alongside a vulnerable `aiohttp`. This is intended.

### Migration note

- This replaces the 0.21.10 advice to run `pip install -U aiohttp` by hand:
  `pip install -U surrealdb-orm` now brings in the patched `aiohttp`.

