## [0.21.18] - 2026-10-27

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One SDK fix (#277, #285), the `v2` port of the
live-stream half of #276 from `main` (0.34.8): `QuerySet.live()` and every
`LiveSelectStream` keep receiving events after a WebSocket reconnect, and
`stop()` ends the re-created live query. No new public API; `live_id` changes
after a reconnect: read *Behaviour change*. The SurrealDB pin is unchanged
(tested against 2.7.0, supported range 2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **`QuerySet.live()` stopped receiving events after its first WebSocket
  reconnect (#277, #285).** This covered every `LiveSelectStream` with
  `auto_resubscribe=True`, the default. The live query was re-created on the
  server with the same filter, but the stream's handler was not registered
  again under the new id and the stream kept the old id, so no event reached
  the iterator. The stream now moves to the re-created live query during the
  resubscribe itself, and a user `on_reconnect` still fires with
  `(old_id, new_id)`. Subscriptions created with their own
  `LiveSubscriptionParams.callback` were not affected.
- **`stop()` after a reconnect left the re-created live query running (#277,
  #285).** It killed the old, already dead live query id, so the re-created
  query kept running and was re-created again on every later reconnect, even
  after the stream had been stopped. `stop()` now ends the current query. This
  also holds when `stop()` begins while the re-created `LIVE SELECT` is still
  in flight, for example an `async with ...live()` block that ends during a
  server restart: the stream refuses the new id and the connection kills that
  query instead of keeping it.

### Behaviour change

- After a reconnect, `LiveSelectStream.live_id` and `LiveModelStream.live_id`
  report the id of the re-created live query (the `new_id` passed to
  `on_reconnect`) instead of the original id, and `stop()` ends that query.
  `LiveSubscriptionParams` keeps its constructor, `repr()` and equality.

### Known limitations

- `LiveSelectManager` still keys each stream by the id `watch()` returned.
  After a reconnect, `active_streams` lists that original id and
  `manager.stop()` must be given it: `stop()` with the new id (the one
  `on_reconnect` receives, and the stream's `live_id`) does nothing, and the
  stream and its re-created live query keep running. `stop_all()` is not
  affected. Before this release the stream was silent after a reconnect, so
  the mismatch had no visible effect.
- Pre-existing, unchanged in this release: the callback API
  (`WebSocketConnection.live(table, callback)` and `LiveQuery.subscribe()`)
  only registers a handler, so after a reconnect its live query is not
  re-created and the callback receives nothing more. A subscription whose
  `LIVE SELECT` fails during the resubscribe (the socket drops again, a
  timeout, a server error) is dropped for good: the next reconnect does not
  retry it, and its stream neither ends nor raises. `main` has the same
  behaviour (#279).

### Upgrading

- Code that stored `live_id` before a reconnect and compares it afterwards
  should take the new id from `on_reconnect` or re-read `live_id`.
- The naive-`datetime` half of #276 is not part of this release; on `v2` it
  is tracked in #258.
- No schema or dependency change; `cbor2` stays `>=5.9.0,<6`.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0: 2,017 unit tests on each of Python 3.12,
3.13 and 3.14, and 560 integration tests, pass; the full integration suite
also passes on SurrealDB 2.6.5 (560), the supported floor. `ruff` and `mypy`
clean. The 17 new tests (14 unit, 3 integration) passed three times in a row
(#285); 13 of them fail on the code before the fix, including all 3
integration tests. The other 4 guard behaviour that does not change
(`on_reconnect` arguments, the callback path, `LiveSubscriptionParams`).

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.18** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

---

## [0.21.17] - 2026-10-27

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One migrations fix (#259, #274), the `v2`
port of #248 from `main` (0.34.6): a generated `AlterField` no longer resets
a field's permissions set outside the ORM. `AlterField` gains two optional
arguments and `FieldState` one attribute to carry the clause; the fix changes
what generated migrations emit: read *Behaviour change* and *Upgrading*. The
SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range
2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **A generated `AlterField` and its rollback reset a field's permissions to
  `FULL` (#259, #274).** Both redefine the field with
  `DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE`, which replaces the whole definition, but they
  re-emitted only the clauses `FieldState` models, and field-level
  `PERMISSIONS` was not one of them. A clause set by hand, by another tool or
  by a `RawSQL` migration fell back to the server default,
  `PERMISSIONS FULL`, on the next generated alteration of that field and
  again on its rollback. The diff now reads the clause from the database
  (new `FieldState.permissions`), and `AlterField` re-emits it, last, in both
  directions through the new `permissions` / `previous_permissions`
  arguments. `main` fixed this with `ALTER FIELD`, which SurrealDB 2.x does
  not parse, so `v2` keeps `DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE` and carries the clause
  instead.
- **`parse_define_field()` read a field's `PERMISSIONS` clause as other
  clauses (#259, #274).** SurrealDB reports a permission's `WHERE` condition
  with field names unescaped, so `FOR select WHERE type = 'public'` was read
  as a `TYPE` clause, and `schema_diff()` raised `ValueError`
  ("Invalid field type") on that field. `default`, `assert`, `comment`,
  `readonly` or `flexible` in a condition set those clauses the same way. A
  preceding `ASSERT $value > 0`, or a regex literal holding an apostrophe
  (`/it's/`), folded the whole clause into the assertion. The clause is now
  split off before the other clauses are read, and a regex literal is read as
  one token.

### Behaviour change

- The diff reads a field's `PERMISSIONS` clause from the database. A
  generated `AlterField` for a field with a non-default permission (`NONE` or
  a `WHERE` condition) ends with that clause, forwards and backwards.
  Statements for fields at the default permissions (`FULL`) are unchanged,
  and `AddField` never emits the clause. `FieldState.__eq__` does not compare
  permissions: models cannot declare field permissions on this line, so a
  permission is never a change for the diff to undo.
- Both arguments are a snapshot: the clause the database had when the
  migration was generated. If a field's permissions change afterwards,
  regenerate the migration (or edit both arguments) before applying it, or
  the captured clause is written back.

### Known limitations

- Hand-written `AlterField` operations and migration files already on disk
  carry no `permissions` / `previous_permissions`, so applying or rolling
  them back still resets the field's permissions to `FULL` (see *Upgrading*).
- `parse_define_field()` is scoped to `INFO FOR TABLE` output, which always
  ends a field definition with a `PERMISSIONS` clause (checked on SurrealDB
  2.6.5 and 2.7.0). Given a hand-written statement with no clause whose
  string literal contains `PERMISSIONS FULL`, `NONE` or `FOR`, such as
  `DEFINE FIELD note ON t TYPE string DEFAULT 'x PERMISSIONS FULL'`, it now
  cuts the body inside that literal (`default` reads `'x`) where it used to
  return the whole literal. Migrations, `schema_diff()` and `inspectdb` read
  definitions from the database and are not affected.
- A `<` or `>` comparison in a `DEFINE FIELD` expression still hides the
  clauses after it, for example `ASSERT $value > 0 COMMENT 'x'` reads the
  comment into the assertion (#273, open; `main` has the same defect). The
  `PERMISSIONS` clause is no longer affected.

### Upgrading

- Before applying or rolling back a migration file generated by an earlier
  version that alters a field with a non-default permission, add
  `permissions` and `previous_permissions` with the field's current clause,
  or regenerate the migration if it has not been applied yet.
- No schema or dependency change; `cbor2` stays `>=5.9.0,<6`.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0 (#274): 2,003 unit tests on each of Python
3.12, 3.13 and 3.14, and 557 integration tests, pass; the full integration
suite also passes on SurrealDB 2.6.5 (557), the supported floor. `ruff` and
`mypy` clean. The 60 new tests (45 unit, 15 integration) passed three times
in a row; 38 of the 45 unit tests fail on the code before the fix.

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.17** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

---

## [0.21.16] - 2026-10-27

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One ORM fix (#261, #271), the `v2` port of
#257 from `main` (0.34.6): the atomic array helpers no longer lose writes
silently, and `atomic_set_add()` no longer adds duplicates. No new public
API, but the fix changes behaviour: read *Behaviour change* and *Upgrading*.
The SurrealDB pin is unchanged (tested against 2.7.0, supported range
2.6.x – 2.7.x).

### Fixed

- **`atomic_set_add()` appended duplicates on an array field (#261, #271).**
  It ran `SET field += $value`, and on SurrealDB 2.x `+=` on an array
  appends, so two calls with the same value left two copies. It now runs
  `array::add(field ?? [], $value)`, which adds only the values the field
  does not already hold. A record without the field, or holding `NULL`, gets
  `[value]` (on SurrealDB 2.7.0, `+=` left a `NULL` field unchanged). On
  SurrealDB 2.x a `set<T>` field holds an array, so the same query covers it.
- **`atomic_append()`, `atomic_remove()` and `atomic_set_add()` returned `[]`
  when SurrealDB rejected the statement (#261, #271)**: a write conflict, a
  failed `ASSERT`, an argument of the wrong type. The write was lost
  silently, indistinguishable from an update that matched no record, and
  `@retry_on_conflict` never saw the conflict it exists to retry. They now
  raise (see *Behaviour change*).
- **`atomic_append()` on a record without the field, or holding `NULL`
  (#261, #271),** was rejected by the server (`array::append()` rejects
  `NONE` and `NULL`), and the rejection was swallowed, so the value was lost.
  It now writes `[value]`. `atomic_remove()` (`-=`) already left a missing
  field alone; its query is unchanged.

### Behaviour change

- `atomic_append()`, `atomic_remove()` and `atomic_set_add()` raise when
  SurrealDB rejects the statement: `TransactionConflictError` for a write
  conflict, which `@retry_on_conflict` retries, and `QueryError` otherwise.
  Both are `SurrealDBError` subclasses from `surreal_sdk` and carry the
  server's message; `QueryError` also carries the query. An `UPDATE` that
  matches no record still returns `[]`.
- `atomic_set_add()` with a list value adds only the elements the field does
  not already hold. On an array field it used to append all of them: `['a']`
  plus `['a', 'b']` gave `['a', 'a', 'b']`, and now gives `['a', 'b']`.
- `atomic_set_add()` on a field holding a number or a string now raises
  `QueryError` (`array::add()` rejects it). On SurrealDB 2.7.0, `+=` added
  to a number (`10` plus `5` gave `15`) and left a string unchanged (`'str'`
  plus `'p1'` stayed `'str'`). Using it on a non-array field was outside the
  documented contract.

### Known limitation

- The choice between `TransactionConflictError` and `QueryError` matches the
  server's message against substrings that include `conflict`. A rejection
  whose message contains that word, such as a failed `ASSERT` echoing a value
  like `'conflict-resolved'` or naming a field like `conflicts`, raises
  `TransactionConflictError`, and `@retry_on_conflict` retries it before
  raising. The matcher is the existing one in `surreal_sdk`, not new in this
  release (same as 0.34.6 on `main`).

### Upgrading

- Code that treated `[]` from these helpers as "the write did not happen"
  should catch `QueryError` / `TransactionConflictError` (or `SurrealDBError`)
  instead, or wrap the call in `@retry_on_conflict` for conflicts.
- Duplicates already written by `atomic_set_add()` are not removed.
- No schema or dependency change; `cbor2` stays `>=5.9.0,<6`.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0 (#271): 1,958 unit tests on each of Python
3.12, 3.13 and 3.14, and 542 integration tests, pass; the full integration
suite also passes on SurrealDB 2.6.5 (542), the supported floor, since the
fix relies on `array::add()` and `??`. `ruff` and `mypy` clean. The 39 new
tests (23 unit, 16 integration) passed three times in a row; 21 of them fail
on the code before the fix.

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.16** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

---

## [0.21.15] - 2026-10-27

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One SDK fix (#231, #254), the `v2` port of
#166 from `main`. No public API change.

### Fixed

- **Datetimes read from SurrealDB 2.x came back as `[seconds, nanoseconds]`
  lists (#231, #254).** Over CBOR, the default protocol, SurrealDB 2.x sends
  every datetime as tag 12 holding a compact `[seconds, nanoseconds]` pair,
  over HTTP and WebSocket alike. The SDK's tag decoder only handled the ISO
  8601 string form, so the pair went through unchanged. A `datetime`-annotated
  model field hid the gap, because the ORM converted the pair afterwards, but
  everything else reached callers as a list of two ints: `raw_query()` and
  `query()` results, datetimes nested in object or `dict` fields, and
  live-query payloads. Two ORM paths broke on it:
  - a `date` model field could not load a datetime stored at midnight UTC:
    it failed validation on the list;
  - a `dict` field loaded from the database and saved back replaced its
    datetimes with `[seconds, nanoseconds]` arrays on the server.

  The pair now decodes to a timezone-aware UTC `datetime`, truncated to the
  microsecond. A value before the epoch decodes too (SurrealDB floors the
  seconds: `1969-12-31T23:59:59.5Z` arrives as `[-1, 500000000]`). The JSON
  protocol is not affected.
- **One undecodable datetime no longer loses the whole message (#231).** An
  ISO datetime string under the datetime tag that Python cannot parse made
  the decoder raise: over HTTP the request failed with `ValueError`, and over
  WebSocket the message was dropped, so the request timed out or the
  live-query notification was lost. That value is now returned as the server
  sent it, and every other value in the same message decodes. A datetime
  outside Python's years 1-9999, which SurrealDB accepts (2.7.0 stores year
  -1), stays the raw `[seconds, nanoseconds]` list.

### Behaviour change

Code that read the raw `[seconds, nanoseconds]` list now receives a
`datetime`, except for a datetime outside years 1-9999.

**Migration:** remove any workaround that converts the list to a `datetime`
or to ISO text yourself. Converting a `datetime` to ISO text is still needed
wherever results are serialised to JSON. No schema or dependency change;
`cbor2` stays `>=5.9.0,<6`.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0 (#254): 1,935 unit tests on each of Python
3.12, 3.13 and 3.14, and 526 integration tests, pass; `ruff` and `mypy`
clean. The tests in the two files #254 touched (70 unit, 16 of them new, and
7 new integration tests) passed three times in a row; 14 of the 23 new tests
fail on the code before the fix, including all 7 integration tests.

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.15** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

---

## [0.21.14] - 2026-10-27

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One SDK fix (#246, #253), the `v2` port of
#239 from `main`. No ORM API change.

### Fixed

- **`live()` filters never matched for some values (#246, #253).**
  SurrealDB 2.x does not evaluate bound parameters in a `LIVE SELECT` `WHERE`
  clause, so `v2` writes the filter values into the query text, and several
  types were written as something other than the value a bound parameter
  carries:
  - a `dict` was written as a quoted Python repr, so it never matched an
    object;
  - a `Decimal` was written as a quoted string, so it never matched a decimal;
  - a record link (`RecordId`, a `ForeignKey` value, a model instance) was
    written as `str(value)`, which is not the same record whenever the table
    or the id is not a plain identifier: the string id `"123"` was read as
    the number 123, for example;
  - a naive `datetime` was written without an offset, which SurrealDB 2.x
    does not parse, so the subscription failed;
  - a string value holding a backslash, or the `$name` of another filter
    parameter, was altered on the way in: values were substituted one key at
    a time with `re.sub`, which read the inserted text as a replacement
    pattern and then scanned it again for the next key.

  Each supported value is now written as the literal of what it binds to as
  a parameter: a `dict` as an object literal with string keys, a `Decimal` as
  `<digits>dec`, a record link as the link to that record (a string id and
  the table between backticks, an integer id as a bare number, since `t:123`
  and ``t:`123` `` are different records), and strings unchanged. A
  `datetime` is written in UTC, and a naive one is read as UTC. The
  parameters are substituted in one pass, so inserted text is never scanned
  again, and a `$name` with no value (`$auth`, `$this`, ...) is left as
  written. Auto-resubscribe after a reconnect goes through the same
  rendering.
- **Binding a `Decimal` or a `datetime.date` made SurrealDB 2.x reject the
  whole request (#246, v2 port of #239).** cbor2 encodes them natively as
  CBOR tags 4 and 1004, which SurrealDB 2.x does not read ("unknown CBOR
  tag"). That covered `save()` of a model with such a field, `exec()` filters
  and `query()` variables. A `Decimal` is now sent as SurrealDB's decimal
  (tag 10, exact digits in positional form) and a `date` as the datetime at
  midnight UTC.
- **A `Decimal` outside SurrealDB's decimal range is refused before the
  request is sent (#246).** Above 79228162514264337593543950335 in magnitude,
  binding it or using it in a `live()` filter raises `ValueError` (over a
  WebSocket connection, `query()` wraps it in the SDK's `ConnectionError`,
  as it does for every encoding error). Binding it was rejected by SurrealDB
  before this release, like any `Decimal`. Places beyond the 28th are
  rounded half away from zero, which is what SurrealDB stores for a longer
  value.

### Behaviour change

A `live()` filter value that has no exact SurrealQL literal now raises
`TypeError`. A `set`, `bytes`, `Duration`, `Table`, an arbitrary object, an
`Enum` that is not a `str` or `int` subclass, a NaN or infinite `Decimal`, or
a `dict` with a key that is not a `str` used to be turned into a quoted
string; a NaN or infinite `float` was written bare (`nan`, `inf`), which
SurrealDB does not read as a number.

**Migration:** convert such a value to a supported type before filtering
(`str`, a number, `Decimal`, `list`, a `dict` with `str` keys, `datetime`,
`date`, `UUID`, `RecordId`): a `set` to a `list`, an `Enum` to its `.value`.
Nothing changes for supported types.

### Known limitations

Not changed by this release; each predates it.

- `live(diff=True)` appends `DIFF` after the `WHERE` clause
  (`LIVE SELECT * FROM t WHERE ... DIFF`). SurrealDB 2.7.0 rejects that as a
  parse error, with or without a `WHERE`; it accepts
  `LIVE SELECT DIFF FROM t WHERE ...`. DIFF-mode subscriptions therefore fail
  on this line. `main` builds the statement the same way.
- A naive `datetime` bound as a query parameter is still refused by the CBOR
  encoder (no default timezone); only `live()` reads it as UTC.
- A `RecordId` whose integer id is outside the signed 64-bit range is
  written as a number SurrealDB reads back as a string id, so it does not
  designate the bound record.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0 (#253): 1894 unit tests on each of Python
3.12, 3.13 and 3.14, and 518 integration tests, pass; `ruff` and `mypy`
clean. The new and changed tests (203 unit, 134 integration) passed three
times in a row, and 149 of them fail on the code before the fix.

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.14** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

---

## [0.21.13] - 2026-10-27

**Bug fix release (V2 LTS).** One SDK fix, backported from `main`.
No public API change.

### Fixed

- **`HTTPTransaction.commit()` renamed parameters a statement did not bind
  (#247, #249; backport of #243).** Over an HTTP connection, `commit()` sends
  every queued statement as one query and namespaces each statement's keys
  (`$k` → `$tx_<i>_k`). It did so with a plain text replace, one key at a
  time, so a key also rewrote every longer `$name` it was a prefix of. The
  renamed name is unbound, and SurrealDB 2.x evaluates it as `NONE` inside the
  transaction without an error: a `WHERE` on it silently matched nothing and a
  `SET` from it silently wrote nothing. Two ways in:
  - a key that prefixes a built-in (`$auth`, `$session`, `$this`, `$before`,
    `$after`, `$value`, `$input`) or another unbound parameter: with key `a`,
    `$auth.id` became `$tx_0_auth.id`, and with key `s`, `$session.ns` became
    `$tx_0_session.ns`. Only names the caller chooses can do this:
    `tx.query()` variables, `extra_vars` used with a
    `SurrealFunc`/`server_values` expression on `save(tx=)`/`merge(tx=)`, and
    `QuerySet.variables()` in `bulk_update(atomic=True)` /
    `bulk_delete(atomic=True)`;
  - a key that is a prefix of `tx_<i>_<an earlier key>` (`t`, `tx`, `tx_`,
    `tx_<i>`, ...; `i` being the statement's position in the transaction,
    so `tx_1` also counts from position 10), which rewrote the references
    already renamed for the keys bound before it (`$tx_0_amount` →
    `$tx_0_tx_0_amount`). The SDK builders `tx.create/update/upsert/relate`
    bind each field under its own name, so `save(tx=)` of a new record,
    `relate(tx=)` (edge data) and `bulk_create(atomic=True)` of a model with
    such a field lost the fields bound before it.

  Only whole `$key` references to a key the statement binds are renamed now,
  in a single pass. `v2` has no `surreal_sdk.utils.substitute_params()`, so the
  fix is a private helper in `surreal_sdk/transaction.py`. Bindings keep their
  `tx_<i>_<key>` names, and a statement the bug did not hit sends the same SQL
  as before. The names the ORM generates for its own bindings (`_sv_*`, `_f*`,
  `_bu*`) could not trigger either case, `delete(tx=)` binds nothing,
  `tx.insert()` prefixes its own keys, and WebSocket transactions do not
  rename. A bound `$k` written inside a quoted string literal is still renamed,
  as before.

### Known limitation

`tx.merge()` over HTTP is still broken, for an unrelated reason this release
does not touch (#251): it emits `MERGE { k = $k, ... }`, which SurrealDB 2.x
does not read as an object. With two or more fields the commit fails with a
parse error; with one field `commit()` returns without an error, but the
server rolls the whole transaction back, its other statements included. That
covers `update(tx=)`, `merge(tx=)` without a `SurrealFunc`, and `save(tx=)` of
a record that is already saved, on an HTTP connection. WebSocket transactions
are not affected.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0 (#249): 1840 unit tests on each of Python 3.12,
3.13 and 3.14, and 385 integration tests, pass; `ruff` and `mypy` clean. Of the
26 new tests, 21 fail on 0.21.12, including the integration test, which
reproduces `$session.ns` evaluating to `NONE` on SurrealDB 2.7.0.

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.13** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

---

## [0.21.12] - 2026-10-27

**Security maintenance release (V2 LTS).** No library code changes vs 0.21.11.
0.21.10 and 0.21.11, which shipped without entries, are backfilled below.

### Security

- **`certifi` 2026.5.20 → 2026.10.23 in `uv.lock` (#236, #237)** —
  GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2 / PYSEC-2026-4320. The 2026.5.20 bundle still trusts
  `emSign ECC Root CA - G3`, under which eight intermediate CA keys were
  compromised (and revoked), so a TLS connection verified against it accepts
  certificates issued with those keys. The two records disagree on the first
  fixed release (2026.10.18 per GHSA, 2026.10.19 per PYSEC, the one `pip-audit`
  reads); 2026.10.23 clears both. That root has been out of the bundle since
  2026.10.18, so 2026.10.23 no longer carries it.
- **Why `v2` lagged:** `certifi` is a transitive dependency (through `httpx`,
  `httpcore` and `requests`), so nothing in `pyproject.toml` moves it, and the
  `v2` lock receives security updates only through the sync of `main`'s
  Dependabot security updates. No `certifi` update had gone through `main`, so
  there was nothing to sync.

### Upgrading

- Upgrading `surrealdb-orm` does not upgrade `certifi`. This package does not
  declare it and nothing it depends on requires a fixed release, so
  `pip install -U surrealdb-orm` and existing lockfiles keep the installed
  bundle. Run `pip install -U certifi` (or bump it in your lockfile) to
  `>= 2026.10.19`.

### Verification

Against a live SurrealDB 2.7.0 (#237): 1815 unit tests on each of Python 3.12,
3.13 and 3.14, and 384 integration tests, pass; `ruff` and `mypy` clean.

### Changed

- **Version bump to 0.21.12** — `pyproject.toml`, `surreal_orm/__init__.py`,
  `surreal_sdk/__init__.py`, `surreal_sdk/pyproject.toml`.

